== Calculations for Melee, Archery, and Throwing Damage ==

:''See also: [[Statistics#Damage Per Second (DPS)]], [[Skill Dual Wield]]''

EverQuest melee damage is made up of several separate mechanics:

# The weapon's base damage.
# The attacker's offensive statistics and the target's mitigation, which determine the variable damage roll.
# Class- and level-dependent damage multipliers.
# A separate '''Main Hand Damage Bonus''' added to eligible attacks.

Some of these mechanics have changed substantially between EverQuest eras.

This page distinguishes between:

* '''Observed EverQuest Legends behavior''' — directly tested in-game.
* '''Working Legends models''' — formulas that closely reproduce observed Legends behavior but have not yet been proven exact.
* '''Inherited / historical mechanics''' — mechanics from Project 1999, EQEmu, or other EverQuest eras that remain useful references but should not be assumed to be identical on Legends.

== Core Damage Structure ==

The exact internal damage-roll formula on EverQuest Legends has not yet been completely reverse-engineered.

However, the working combat model separates the variable weapon-damage roll from the '''Main Hand Damage Bonus'''.

In simplified form:

<pre>
Variable Weapon Damage
    = function(
        Weapon Damage,
        Weapon Skill,
        STR,
        ATK,
        Target Mitigation,
        Damage Roll
      )

Final Damage
    = Variable Weapon Damage
    + Main Hand Damage Bonus
</pre>

This distinction is important.

'''STR and ATK do not directly increase the displayed Main Hand Damage Bonus.'''

Instead, STR, ATK, weapon skill, and the target's defensive statistics affect the variable weapon-damage portion of the attack. The weapon damage bonus is then treated as a separate flat contribution to an eligible primary-hand attack.

The inherited EQ combat model likewise applies the Main Hand Damage Bonus only after calculating the variable <code>DamageDone</code> value. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

== Main Hand Damage Bonus on EverQuest Legends ==

EverQuest Legends does '''not''' appear to use the older classic-era damage-bonus progression previously documented on this wiki.

In particular, the old one-handed formula:

<pre>
floor((Level - 25) / 3)
</pre>

does not reproduce observed Legends values.

Damage Bonus on Legends has been observed to depend on:

* Character level.
* Weapon delay.
* Whether the weapon is one-handed or two-handed.
* Potentially weapon damage itself.

