== Working Legends Damage Bonus Formula ==

The Legends observations closely resemble a later-era EverQuest damage-bonus formula.

The current calculator therefore uses this as its '''working Legends model''':

<pre>
Damage Bonus Raw =
    Hand Modifier
    × max(Character Level, Weapon Damage)
    × (min(Weapon Delay, 50) / 40)
    × (Character Level / 100)
</pre>

Where:

{| class="eoTable3" style="text-align:center"
|-
! Weapon Type
! Hand Modifier
|-
| One-Handed || '''0.8'''
|-
| Two-Handed || '''1.1'''
|}

The delay portion is capped at:

<pre>
50 delay
</pre>

so the working model uses:

<pre>
min(Weapon Delay, 50)
</pre>

rather than allowing delay scaling to increase indefinitely.

=== Weapon Damage May Also Affect Damage Bonus ===

The formula uses:

<pre>
max(Character Level, Weapon Damage)
</pre>

This means:

* If weapon damage is below the character's level, character level controls this part of the formula.
* If weapon damage exceeds character level, the weapon's damage value may increase the Damage Bonus.

This portion of the formula has '''not yet been directly isolated on Legends'''.

Several of the current observations were recorded by delay and displayed Damage Bonus without also recording the weapon's DMG value, so the <code>max(Level, Damage)</code> behavior cannot yet be fully verified from the current dataset.

=== The Formula Is a Working Model, Not Yet Exact ==

The later-era formula matches much of the level-49 Legends dataset extremely closely.

For example, it reproduces or nearly reproduces the observed progression across:

* 22-40 delay one-handed weapons.
* 30-58 delay two-handed weapons.
* The substantially larger two-handed Damage Bonus compared with one-handed weapons.

However, the full Legends implementation is '''not yet proven'''.

In particular:

* The observed 24-delay progression across levels 32, 48, 49, and 50 is not perfectly explained by one simple integer-rounding rule.
* The level-49 70-delay two-handed observation is higher than a simple delay-capped calculation would predict unless another input, such as weapon damage, affects the result.
* Exact rounding or truncation behavior remains unknown.
* Legends may contain additional conditions or adjustments not present in the known later-era reference formula.

For this reason, the formula should currently be described as:

'''The best-supported working model for EverQuest Legends Damage Bonus.'''

It should not yet be described as the exact server formula.

== Comparing Weapons ==

At low levels, raw weapon ratio is useful:

<pre>
Weapon Ratio = Damage / Delay
</pre>

At levels where Damage Bonus is significant, raw ratio becomes incomplete.

A useful normalized comparison is:

<pre>
Normalized Primary Score =
    ((2 × Weapon Damage) + Damage Bonus)
    / Weapon Delay
</pre>

The <code>2 × Weapon Damage</code> term is a traditional comparison benchmark. It should not be interpreted as the literal average damage of every hit.

=== Dual Wield ==

EverQuest Legends has been directly observed to use '''independent primary and secondary weapon timers'''.

The primary weapon and secondary weapon each advance according to their own delay.

A fast secondary weapon can therefore reach multiple offhand attack opportunities before a slower primary weapon reaches its next attack.

The secondary weapon's attack opportunity is additionally gated by the Dual Wield check.

The working normalized comparison is:

<pre>
Main-Hand Score =
    ((2 × Main Damage) + Main Weapon Damage Bonus)
    / Main Delay

Offhand Score =
    Dual Wield Chance
    × ((2 × Offhand Damage) / Offhand Delay)

Dual Wield Score =
    Main-Hand Score
    + Offhand Score
</pre>

The secondary hand receives no Main Hand Damage Bonus under the current working model.

Because the Main Hand Damage Bonus itself depends on weapon damage and delay, the calculator tests both possible arrangements:

* Weapon A primary / Weapon B secondary.
* Weapon B primary / Weapon A secondary.

The stronger arrangement is then compared against the two-handed weapon.

See [[Skill Dual Wield]] for the current Dual Wield chance model and Legends-specific research.

== Working Melee Damage Roll ==

The following model is inherited from historical EverQuest / EQEmu research and remains the best current framework for understanding the variable portion of melee damage.

'''The exact constants have not yet been independently verified on EverQuest Legends.'''

=== 1. Calculate Offensive Power ("Wrath") ===

<pre>
StrengthModifier =
    ((2 × STR) - 150) / 3

If STR < 75:
    StrengthModifier = 0

Wrath =
    Weapon Skill
    + StrengthModifier
    + Worn ATK
    + Spell ATK
</pre>

This is why STR and ATK affect actual melee damage even though they do not directly alter the flat weapon Damage Bonus.

=== 2. Determine the Damage Roll ===

The inherited model uses an offense-versus-mitigation roll that ultimately produces one of twenty damage intervals.

A simplified version is:

<pre>
WrathRoll =
    random(0.0, 1.0)
    × (Wrath + 5)

MitigationRoll =
    random(0.0, 1.0)
    × (
        Target Worn AC
        + Target Spell AC
        + Target Defense / 5
        + Target Agility / 20
        + 5
      )

AverageRoll =
    (WrathRoll + MitigationRoll + 10)
    / 2

RollIndex =
    (WrathRoll - MitigationRoll)
    + (AverageRoll / 2)

Weighted D20 =
    floor(
        RollIndex
        × 20
        / AverageRoll
    )

Weighted D20 is constrained to 0-19,
then increased by 1.

RolledD20 =
    Weighted D20 / 10
</pre>

The precise Legends distribution remains to be verified.

=== 3. Calculate Variable Weapon Damage ===

The inherited model applies an additional player damage multiplier when offensive power is sufficiently high.

Simplified:

<pre>
If Wrath < 115:

    DamageDone =
        Weapon Damage
        × RolledD20

Otherwise:

    Roll against maxExtraChance.

    If the extra roll succeeds:

        BaseBonus =
            (Wrath - minusFactor) / 2

        BaseBonus has a minimum of 10.

        ExtraPercent =
            100
            + (
                BaseBonus
                × random(0.0, 1.0)
              )

        ExtraPercent is capped at maxExtra.

        DamageDone =
            Weapon Damage
            × RolledD20
            × ExtraPercent
            / 100
</pre>

=== 4. Add Main Hand Damage Bonus ===

After the variable weapon-damage calculation:

<pre>
FinalDamage =
    DamageDone
    + Main Hand Damage Bonus
</pre>

This separation is important when evaluating Damage Bonus.

Damage Bonus does '''not''' simply increase the weapon's listed DMG stat before STR, ATK, mitigation, and the variable damage roll are applied.

The inherited combat documentation likewise places Main Hand Damage Bonus at the end of the damage calculation. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}

