The original EQEmu implementation contains class-specific handling, including Monk-specific behavior, so unique Legends class tables remain possible.

=== Archery Damage Bonus ===

Historical EverQuest implementations have allowed Archery to use the Main Hand Damage Bonus of the weapon equipped in the primary slot.

This has '''not yet been directly confirmed on Legends'''.

The inherited model also includes special Ranger treatment for stationary targets.

Both mechanics should be tested before being presented as confirmed Legends rules.

=== Throwing Damage Bonus ===

Historical implementations do '''not''' apply Main Hand Damage Bonus to Throwing attacks.

This remains unverified on Legends.

=== Critical Hits ===

The inherited critical-hit model applies critical modifications to the variable <code>DamageDone</code> value before adding the Main Hand Damage Bonus.

Historical simplified logic includes:

<pre>
If a critical hit occurs:

    DamageDone =
        max(DamageDone, Weapon Damage)

    DamageDone =
        DamageDone + 5

    DamageDone =
        DamageDone × 1.7

Then:

    FinalDamage =
        DamageDone
        + Main Hand Damage Bonus
</pre>

Warrior Berserk criticals have historically received an additional modifier.

Exact Legends critical behavior remains to be tested.

== Legends Verification Priorities ==

The highest-value remaining melee tests are:

# '''Damage Bonus formula''' — record Level, weapon DMG, Delay, handedness, and displayed Damage Bonus together.
# '''Damage Bonus rounding''' — test adjacent delays and levels where the working formula produces fractional results near integer boundaries.
# '''High-DMG weapons''' — test weapons whose listed damage exceeds character level to verify the <code>max(Level, Damage)</code> portion.
# '''Delay above 50''' — test multiple 2H weapons above 50 delay while also recording weapon DMG to determine why the 70-delay observation differs from the simplest capped-delay prediction.
# '''Damage Bonus activation level''' — test several weapons immediately below and above level 28.
# '''Offhand Damage Bonus''' — verify directly that an ordinary secondary-hand attack receives no Damage Bonus on Legends.
# '''Damage multiplier''' — parse large samples against a controlled target to determine whether Legends uses the inherited <code>maxExtra</code> progression.
# '''Damage caps''' — test very high-DMG weapons at low levels to determine whether inherited class-specific weapon caps apply.
# '''Archery and Throwing''' — confirm whether their historical Main Hand Damage Bonus behavior remains present.

